186045417] [17592186420011 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420011 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e6571d0-2b85-5e20-ad0d-0458eb1f4ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "637ffaff-c65f-5c9b-99c9-53a0db0e19a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420021 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae1135571904a4bfb49eb79e187fa6d1754fc562fe66b604d33eb555418a1345.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/1515f63552e99b9ca9e1780f9689ac5d148969aaa1dccfc2ba55bcb87e534b34.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d20e4de9-7a35-5187-8874-829d308828ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420024 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422961 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422961 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "083834f0-9bf1-5b96-ab34-e2a726a19d0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422966 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "329126be-3296-51e6-af07-1508950c1b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422974 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422974 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422974 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "879273c7-bb2b-55dd-b5d2-dbaa262517cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422975 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422975 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422975 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e8c52e8-48bd-5ead-ba09-dce5bd0183da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422976 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186422976 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422976 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "96647981-19ea-5f8c-a90f-017ed2cbe4a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428988